  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 PM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES water vapor imagery reveals vertically stacked \nlow spinning over eastern Lake Superior. Differential vorticity \nadvection combined with meager surface based instability has been \nthe driver of scattered shower and thunderstorm activity this \nafternoon, with the greatest concentration of thunderstorms (non-\nsevere) in the east half from ISQ to Whitefish Point. Expecting this \nactivity to persist this afternoon before diminishing altogether as \ndiurnal instability wanes tonight and the upper-low moves into \nsouthern Ontario. Some lingering upslope light rain and or drizzle \nmay be present tonight in the west half NW orographic lift areas. \nOtherwise, temps fall into the 50s tonight. Northwest flow aloft and \nbuilding surface high pressure will usher in a cooler and drier day \ntomorrow as the Great Lakes become sandwiched between a descending \ntrough near James Bay and a building ridge in the High Plains. Under \nbreezy N to NW winds, temps in the upper 60s will be common across \nthe north half while the southern UP peaks into the low 70s. Under \nmostly clear skies and calming winds tomorrow night into Friday \nmorning, efficient radiational cooling may be able to produce low \ntemperatures in the 40s to upper 30s for areas of the interior UP. \nHave opted to bide on the cool side of guidance (NBM 10th \npercentile) for this forecast package.\n\nHeight rises and W to SW mid-level flow brings seasonably warm \ntemperatures and additional shortwave activity in the \nSaturday/Sunday timeframe to early parts of next week. No one day \nlooks like a guarantee wash-out, but chances for showers and \nthunderstorms can be expected.",
